  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "The outbox pattern, already built",
      "items": [
        "the write and its after-effect must commit together — hand-rolled outbox tables, polling loops and janitor goroutines are the canonical NOT-built-here example now that River ships the mechanism",
        "riverClient.AddTx(ctx, river.AddTxArgs{Tx: tx, ...}) enqueues inside the SAME Postgres transaction as the sqlc write — no dual-write gap, at-least-once by construction"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Workers, not loops",
      "items": [
        "work is a job struct with a Kind() (+ Validate), handed to a river.WorkerDefaults worker; no poll intervals, no SKIP LOCKED CTE, no leases to tune",
        "cmd/worker runs the River client: queues for email and webhooks; river.PeriodicJobs (cron spec) covers the old janitor sweeps like purging expired sessions and codes"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "Durability",
      "items": [
        "UniqueOpts dedupes resubmits so re-signup doesn't double-email; failures follow River's backoff and discard after the configured max attempts, visible in river_job and slog rows",
        "the client drains in-flight jobs on SIGTERM — the same graceful-shutdown context discipline as the API server; migrations run with golang-migrate as a deploy step, never at worker startup"
      ]
    }
  ]
}